      "filename": "Breaux Bridge, Louisiana (4).jpg",
      "title": "File:Breaux Bridge, Louisiana (4).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0d/Breaux_Bridge%2C_Louisiana_%284%29.jpg",
      "thumbUrl": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Breaux_Bridge%2C_Louisiana_%284%29.jpg/960px-Breaux_Bridge%2C_Louisiana_%284%29.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Breaux_Bridge,_Louisiana_(4).jpg",
      "width": 2816,
      "height": 2112,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0",
      "artist": "Ken Lund",
      "description": "Breaux Bridge is a village in St. Martin Parish, Louisiana, United States. The population was 7,281 at the 2000 census. It is part of the Lafayette Metropolitan Statistical Area.\nThis community is called the \"Crawfish Capital of the World\", a designation obtained by former Speaker of the Louisiana House of Representatives Robert J. \"Bob\" Angelle. It is also known for its unusual listing of nicknames in its telephone directory.\nIn 1771, Acadian pioneer Firmin Breaux bought land in present-day city of Breaux Bridge. He purchased the land from Jean François Ledée, a wealthy New Orleans merchant who had acquired the land as a French land grant. By 1774, Breaux's branding iron was registered and by 1786 he was one of the largest property owners in the Bayou Teche country.\nIn 1799, Breaux built a footbridge across the Bayou Teche to help ease the passage for his family and neighbors. This first bridge was a suspension footbridge, likely made of rope and small planks. It was stabilized by being tied to small pilings located at each end of the bridge, as well as to a pair of huge live oak trees on both sides of the bayou. When traveling directions were given, residents would often instruct people to \"go to Breaux's bridge...\", which eventually was adopted as the city's name[citation needed].\nIn 1817, Firmin's son Agricole built the first vehicular bridge, allowing for the passage of wagons and increased commerce in the area. The town received its official founding in 1829 when Scholastique Picou Breaux, Agricole's widow, drew up a plan called Plan de la Ville Du Pont des Breaux for the city and began developing the property by selling lots to other Acadian settlers. A church parish was created in 1847 and in 1859, Breaux Bridge was officially incorporated. \nIn 1959, the Louisiana legislature officially designated Breaux Bridge as \"la capitale Mondiale de l'ecrevisse\" (\"the Crawfish Capital of the World\").\n\nhttp://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Breaux_Bridge,_Louisiana",
      "dateCreated": "2009-09-10 15:51:22",
      "sourceType": "sibling",
      "sourceArticleId": "external:Acadiana",
